WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.244
The event library is written in Kotlin.

2
00:00:05.244 --> 00:00:08.656
A Java team in the same service wants to call it too.

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.444
javac can't read Kotlin.

4
00:00:10.444 --> 00:00:13.115
It reads the class files kotlinc wrote.

5
00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:17.225
Some Kotlin features look odd there, or aren't there at all.

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.224
Start with a constructor with default arguments.

7
00:00:21.224 --> 00:00:24.179
From Kotlin, PlainBus of refunds just works.

8
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:28.259
From Java, the same call fails: no suitable constructor found.

9
00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:32.944
The class file has only two: the one with every default, and the full one.

10
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:37.888
@JvmOverloads tells kotlinc to write the ones in between.

11
00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:39.973
Now Java's call compiles.

12
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:41.960
Next, subscribe.

13
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.643
Kotlin callers use the reified one.

14
00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:46.559
Java can't see it at all.

15
00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:51.235
It's compiled as a synthetic method, and javac ignores those.

16
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.448
The KClass one works from Java, if Java converts the class by hand.

17
00:00:56.448 --> 00:00:57.579
It reads badly.

18
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.479
The fix is an overload that takes a Java Class, and converts it for you.

19
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.841
From Java, an object member goes through INSTANCE, and a companion val through Companion and a getter.

20
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.867
@JvmStatic makes publish a real static method.

21
00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:17.565
@JvmField makes SYSTEM a plain static field.

22
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.859
Last, the event itself.

23
00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:25.192
Its id and amount are value classes, and their init blocks check every value.

24
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:28.689
In the class file, a value class is just what it wraps.

25
00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:34.669
So Java sees a private constructor that takes a String and a long, and getters with mangled names.

26
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:39.990
A @JvmName shortcut would let Java pass a raw long straight past init.

27
00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.112
The facade does the opposite.

28
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:48.463
It takes plain values, and builds the value classes itself, so every init block runs.

29
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.207
A good call goes through.

30
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.656
Predict.

31
00:01:52.656 --> 00:01:57.683
A Java caller passes 7781, with no pay_ prefix.

32
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.073
What happens?

33
00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:05.175
PaymentId's init throws, exactly as it would for a Kotlin caller.

34
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.271
Here is the facade in Kotlin, and a Java caller.

35
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:12.959
The rule.

36
00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:15.266
Java sees only the class file.

37
00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:22.804
Give it Class overloads, @JvmOverloads, statics, and a facade that still builds the value classes.

38
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.158
Check yourself.

39
00:02:24.158 --> 00:02:27.015
There's no @JvmOverloads on PlainBus.

40
00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:31.724
Does new PlainBus, with a name and a flag, compile from Java?

41
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:34.617
Yes.

42
00:02:34.617 --> 00:02:37.237
The full constructor is always there.

43
00:02:37.237 --> 00:02:39.857
Only the ones in between are missing.
